WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.323
What benchmarks miss.

2
00:00:01.603 --> 00:00:06.531
Even a clean, unsaturated benchmark leaves out most of what makes work hard.

3
00:00:06.811 --> 00:00:11.845
METR’s own page lists several gaps for its suite, and they generalize: Messiness.

4
00:00:12.125 --> 00:00:16.029
Benchmark tasks have clear specifications and automatic checks.

5
00:00:16.309 --> 00:00:22.048
Real tasks have ambiguous goals, missing context, and stakeholders who change their minds.

6
00:00:22.328 --> 00:00:29.752
METR reports agents did worse on messier tasks and when scored holistically by people rather than by algorithmic checks.

7
00:00:30.032 --> 00:00:32.016
Reliability at business levels.

8
00:00:32.296 --> 00:00:36.712
A 50% success rate is a research milestone and a product failure.

9
00:00:36.992 --> 00:00:41.963
Most work needs 95% or better, plus a way to know which 5% failed.

10
00:00:42.243 --> 00:00:43.288
Cost and time.

11
00:00:43.568 --> 00:00:48.368
Benchmarks rarely report what a success cost in tokens or wall-clock time.

12
00:00:48.648 --> 00:01:02.664
Kapoor and colleagues (“AI Agents That Matter”, 2024) argued that agent evaluations should report cost alongside accuracy, because a small gain bought with ten times the compute may not be worth it.

13
00:01:02.944 --> 00:01:04.139
Domain transfer.

14
00:01:04.419 --> 00:01:10.904
Software tasks have checkable answers, which is exactly where RL training concentrated (lesson 1).

15
00:01:11.184 --> 00:01:17.712
Strong coding horizons say little about, for example, writing a policy memo that a regulator will accept.

16
00:01:17.992 --> 00:01:19.400
Organizational fit.

17
00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:27.787
Whether a team actually gets faster depends on review load, integration, and trust, which lesson 6 examines with field evidence.

18
00:01:28.067 --> 00:01:32.163
The gap between benchmark and work is not a reason to ignore benchmarks.

19
00:01:32.443 --> 00:01:37.093
It is a reason to treat them as a shortlist and to measure the last mile yourself.
